<TopicPage slug="subjects/archaeology/foundational/what-archaeology-is">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Archaeology is the study of the human past through its material remains — everything people
    left behind, from mammoth-bone huts to sweet wrappers. It is the only discipline with a
    million-year perspective on humanity, and it runs on a sobering rule: excavation destroys the
    site, so the record taken must outlast the site itself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the method, from survival to inference" widget="equationStep" config={methodsCfg} />

  <h2>Context is the science</h2>
  <p>
    An artefact ripped from its layer is a curiosity; in its layer, it is evidence. Provenance —
    exactly where and with what an object was found — is what lets archaeologists argue about
    trade, ritual or diet. This is why looting is not theft of objects but destruction of
    knowledge, and why the antiquities market and archaeology are permanent enemies.
  </p>

  <h2>The deeper past keeps getting deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The discipline's frontier is constantly renegotiated by science: ancient DNA read from bones
    now rewrites migrations, isotopes in teeth reveal where individuals grew up, and lidar strips
    jungle canopy to reveal cities no surveyor ever walked. The interpretive questions — inequality,
    belief, collapse — stay old; the microscopes keep getting newer.
  </p>

  <h2>Whose past is it?</h2>
  <p>
    Archaeology is also a present-day institution with obligations: indigenous communities
    reclaiming ancestors and objects, museums confronting collections, laws (from the US NAGPRA to
    the UNESCO conventions) regulating dug and exported heritage. Managing that — cultural
    resource management — is now most archaeologists' actual job, and its ethics are part of the
    science, not an appendix to it.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Renfrew &amp; Bahn, <em>Archaeology: Theories, Methods and Practice</em>; Harris, <em>Principles of Archaeological Stratigraphy</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
